Aim for stretches of at least an hour where you can, since shorter sessions interrupt the deep sleep that makes the practice valuable. Choose a chair with good back support, keep water within reach, and let someone else settle you in with a wrap so you are not adjusting anything once your baby has settled.

Partners can do this too, and it matters that they do. The benefit comes from the closeness, warmth and stillness, not from who is providing it.', 'Bonding', 4, 'Ibibondo', '2026-07-10', 'lavender'),
  ('a2', 'feeding-a-preterm-baby', 'Feeding a preterm baby: what changes', 'Smaller stomachs, less stamina and a suck-swallow reflex still under construction.', 'A baby born early tires quickly during feeds and may not yet coordinate sucking, swallowing and breathing reliably. Expect feeds to be smaller and more frequent than the schedules written for term babies.

A slow-flow teat gives your baby time to manage each mouthful. Watch for the signs that a feed is going well: steady rhythmic sucking, pauses that your baby recovers from, and calm colour throughout.

If your baby consistently falls asleep within a few minutes, loses colour, or feeds take longer than about forty minutes, raise it with your health provider rather than pushing through.', 'Feeding', 5, 'Ibibondo', '2026-07-06', 'mint'),
  ('a3', 'safe-sleep-basics', 'Safe sleep, simply explained', 'Five rules that matter more than any product you can buy.', 'Put your baby on their back for every sleep, day and night, until their first birthday. Use a firm flat mattress with a fitted sheet and nothing else in the cot — no pillows, bumpers, loose blankets or soft toys.

A wearable sleep sack solves the blanket problem: your baby stays warm and cannot end up underneath anything. Keep the room comfortable rather than hot, and dress your baby in one more layer than you would wear yourself.

Share a room for the first six months, but not a bed. Room-sharing is associated with lower risk; bed-sharing is not.', 'Sleep', 3, 'Ibibondo', '2026-07-01', 'sky'),
  ('a4', 'caring-for-delicate-skin', 'Caring for very delicate skin', 'Why less is genuinely more in the first months.', 'Newborn skin — and preterm skin especially — is thinner than adult skin and absorbs more of what is put on it. That is the whole argument for keeping a skincare routine short.

Bathe two or three times a week rather than daily, in water that feels warm rather than hot, for no more than about five minutes. Between baths, topping and tailing with plain water is enough.

Choose fragrance-free products without dyes. Fragrance is the most common cause of irritation, and nothing about it benefits your baby.', 'Skincare', 4, 'Ibibondo', '2026-06-24', 'pink'),
  ('a5', 'tracking-weight-gain', 'Tracking weight gain without the worry', 'What the numbers mean, and when they actually matter.', 'Most babies lose some weight in the first days and are back to birth weight within about two weeks. After that, steady gain along a consistent curve matters far more than any single reading.

Weigh at the same time of day, on the same scale, with your baby undressed. Weighing daily tends to produce anxiety rather than information — weekly is usually plenty unless you have been asked to do otherwise.

Bring the trend, not one number, to your appointments. A line on a chart tells your provider much more than a single measurement.', 'Growth', 4, 'Ibibondo', '2026-06-18', 'sunny'),
  ('a6', 'preparing-to-come-home', 'Preparing to bring your baby home', 'A practical checklist for the week before discharge.', 'Ask the ward what your baby is currently feeding on, how often, and what to do if a feed is refused. Write the answers down — discharge day is not when you will remember them.

Get clothing that fits now, not in a month. Preterm sizes exist for a reason, and a baby swamped in newborn clothes is harder to keep warm and harder to monitor.

Set up one comfortable feeding spot with everything within arm’s reach, and agree who is doing the night shifts before the first night rather than during it.', 'Coming home', 5, 'Ibibondo', '2026-06-12', 'mint');


